{"shortDescription":"Guggenheim Securities launched coverage of 22 aerospace and defense companies with a bullish view of defense contractors and aircraft manufacturers. The firm is more cautious about commercial aerospace aftermarket suppliers as passenger traffic weakens and airlines face higher fuel costs.The analys…","astDescription":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"p","children":["Guggenheim Securities launched coverage of 22 aerospace and defense companies with a bullish view of defense contractors and aircraft manufacturers. The firm is more cautious about commercial aerospace aftermarket suppliers as passenger traffic weakens and airlines face higher fuel costs."]},{"type":"p","children":["The analysts said defense stocks have fallen about 25% from their March 2026 peak, while forward valuation multiples have contracted roughly 55%. Guggenheim views the decline as a buying opportunity because geopolitical threats, weapons replenishment and military modernization should support spending into the next decade."]},{"type":"p","children":[{"type":"b","children":["$1 trillion budget could support growth"]}]},{"type":"p","children":["The firm expects the fiscal 2027 base defense budget to reach approximately $1 trillion. Investment accounts could approach $600 billion, or more than 43% of total defense spending. That compares with 32% in fiscal 2013."]},{"type":"p","children":["For investors, the central argument is that defense companies now combine lower valuations with unusually strong revenue visibility. Publicly traded contractors’ backlogs rose 25% from a year earlier during the second quarter and 42% from the second quarter of 2024, according to Guggenheim. Expansion of manufacturing capacity could also accelerate sales as contractors replenish missile inventories and deploy newer technologies."]},{"type":"p","children":["Guggenheim initiated Buy ratings on Applied Aerospace \u0026 Defense ",{"type":"symbol","params":{"symbol":"NYSE:AADX","text":"NYSE:AADX"}},", BWX Technologies ",{"type":"symbol","params":{"symbol":"NYSE:BWXT","text":"NYSE:BWXT"}},", Curtiss-Wright ",{"type":"symbol","params":{"symbol":"NYSE:CW","text":"NYSE:CW"}},", Leonardo DRS ",{"type":"symbol","params":{"symbol":"NASDAQ:DRS","text":"NASDAQ:DRS"}},", Karman Holdings ",{"type":"symbol","params":{"symbol":"NYSE:KRMN","text":"NYSE:KRMN"}},", Kratos Defense ",{"type":"symbol","params":{"symbol":"NASDAQ:KTOS","text":"NASDAQ:KTOS"}},", L3Harris Technologies ",{"type":"symbol","params":{"symbol":"NYSE:LHX","text":"NYSE:LHX"}},", Lyntris ",{"type":"symbol","params":{"symbol":"NYSE:LYNX","text":"NYSE:LYNX"}},", Mercury Systems ",{"type":"symbol","params":{"symbol":"NASDAQ:MRCY","text":"NASDAQ:MRCY"}}," and Northrop Grumman ",{"type":"symbol","params":{"symbol":"NYSE:NOC","text":"NYSE:NOC"}},". The analysts’ price targets imply average upside of about 38%."]},{"type":"p","children":["Applied Aerospace ",{"type":"symbol","params":{"symbol":"NYSE:AADX","text":"NYSE:AADX"}}," carries the largest projected return, with a $30 target that represents 143% upside from the price cited in the report. Other notable calls include Karman ",{"type":"symbol","params":{"symbol":"NYSE:KRMN","text":"NYSE:KRMN"}},", with 79% potential upside, Lyntris ",{"type":"symbol","params":{"symbol":"NYSE:LYNX","text":"NYSE:LYNX"}}," with 60% and Kratos ",{"type":"symbol","params":{"symbol":"NASDAQ:KTOS","text":"NASDAQ:KTOS"}}," with 58%. Guggenheim rated Moog (",{"type":"url","params":{"url":"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/MOG.A?utm_source=tradingview\u0026utm_medium=referral\u0026feed_item_type=news","linkText":"MOG.A"}},")(",{"type":"url","params":{"url":"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/MOG.B?utm_source=tradingview\u0026utm_medium=referral\u0026feed_item_type=news","linkText":"MOG.B"}},"), Redwire ",{"type":"symbol","params":{"symbol":"NYSE:RDW","text":"NYSE:RDW"}}," and York Space Systems ",{"type":"symbol","params":{"symbol":"NYSE:YSS","text":"NYSE:YSS"}}," as Neutral."]},{"type":"p","children":[{"type":"b","children":["L3Harris offers a restructuring opportunity"]}]},{"type":"p","children":["L3Harris ",{"type":"symbol","params":{"symbol":"NYSE:LHX","text":"NYSE:LHX"}}," is Guggenheim’s differentiated large-cap recommendation. The firm assigned the company a $365 price target, implying 49% upside from the report’s reference price of $246. Guggenheim believes asset sales, business separations and industry consolidation could unlock value beyond the benefits of higher defense spending."]},{"type":"p","children":["The analysts acknowledged several risks to their defense thesis. These include the November elections, the federal deficit, higher interest rates and uncertainty surrounding supplemental spending. A continuing resolution also funds the government only through Dec. 11, leaving major budget decisions until after the elections."]},{"type":"p","children":[{"type":"b","children":["Aircraft production and aftermarket prospects diverge"]}]},{"type":"p","children":["Guggenheim is also bullish on suppliers tied to new aircraft production. It expects Boeing ",{"type":"symbol","params":{"symbol":"NYSE:BA","text":"NYSE:BA"}}," production to grow at an 11% compound annual rate through 2030, including 15% growth for wide-body aircraft and 10% for narrow-body jets. The firm rates Howmet Aerospace ",{"type":"symbol","params":{"symbol":"NYSE:HWM","text":"NYSE:HWM"}},", Hexcel ",{"type":"symbol","params":{"symbol":"NYSE:HXL","text":"NYSE:HXL"}},", RBC Bearings ",{"type":"symbol","params":{"symbol":"NYSE:RBC","text":"NYSE:RBC"}}," and Woodward ",{"type":"symbol","params":{"symbol":"NASDAQ:WWD","text":"NASDAQ:WWD"}}," as Buy, with average potential upside of 31%."]},{"type":"p","children":["The outlook is less favorable for aftermarket companies. Guggenheim forecasts global passenger traffic growth of only 1.8% in 2026, down from 5.4% last year. Higher fares, slower traffic and additional aircraft retirements could weaken parts demand within six to 12 